 "stimulus_html": "<p>“I attach more importance to these deep incisions into the enemy’s country, because this war differs from European wars in this particular: we are not only fighting hostile armies, but a hostile people, and must make old and young, rich and poor, feel the hard hand of war, as well as their organized armies. I know that this recent movement of mine through Georgia has had a wonderful effect in this respect. Thousands who had been deceived by their lying newspapers to believe that we were being whipped all the time now realize the truth, and have no appetite for a repetition of the same experience. . . . But I think faith in him [Jefferson Davis] is much shaken in Georgia, and before we have done with her South Carolina will not be quite so tempestuous. . . . When I move, the Fifteenth Corps will be on the right of the right wing, and their position will naturally bring them into Charleston first; and, if you have watched the history of that corps, you will have remarked that they generally do their work pretty well. The truth is, the whole army is burning with an insatiable desire to wreak vengeance upon South Carolina. I almost tremble at her fate, but feel that she deserves all that seems in store for her.”</p>\n<p align=\"right\">General William T. Sherman, “Letter to General Henry Halleck,” December 24, 1864</p>\n<p><i>Refer to the excerpt provided.</i></p>",
 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 615845,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "The most significant reason for General Sherman’s explanation of hard war in the excerpt was that",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 1171346,
     "content_html": "until 1864, the Confederacy had been winning the Civil War"
    },
    {
     "id": 1171349,
     "content_html": "it was necessary to make the entire Confederate population, not just the military, feel the pain of war in order to defeat the rebellion"
    },
    {
     "id": 1171347,
     "content_html": "the Confederate army had used the same strategy in its invasion of Maryland and the Union wanted revenge"
    },
    {
     "id": 1171348,
     "content_html": "the nonmilitary population in the South needed to be rescued from Confederate oppressors"
    }
   ],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "free-response",
    "multiple-choice"
   ],
   "combo_choices": []
  },
  {
   "id": 615846,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "Which of the statements is the most accurate paraphrase of General Sherman’s statement, “Thousands who had been deceived by their lying newspapers to believe that we were being whipped all the time now realize the truth, and have no appetite for a repetition of the same experience”?",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 1171353,
     "content_html": "Georgia citizens who had believed the South was winning the war were beginning to understand the strength of the Union army."
    },
    {
     "id": 1171350,
     "content_html": "Newspaper editors who published false reports had been whipped in the streets as an example to others."
    },
    {
     "id": 1171351,
     "content_html": "Southerners had experienced such severe shortage of food and consumer items that they had come to have no appetite."
    },
    {
     "id": 1171352,
     "content_html": "This war was similar to European wars in that all the fighting was carried out by organized armies."
    }
   ],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
